  "query_preview": "..Sunday, Oct 18 through Friday, Oct 23...As the high\ncontinues its eastward shuffle, winds continue to weaken late\nSaturday into Sunday, so while winds will be offshore Sunday\nmorning, they will be much weaker. Winds will switch to onshore\nSunday afternoon, bring additional cooling, a trend that will\ncontinue into Monday as weak low pressure hangs out in southern \nCalifornia. At least patchy marine layer low clouds will likely\nreturn Monday morning, resulting in patchy coastal fog.\n\nA large- scale, stronger mid- level low pressure system starts \nmoving down from the Gulf of Alaska late Monday into Tuesday, \nbringing breezier onshore flow and even cooler temperatures for \nTuesday, along with a chance for showers that will increase from \nnorth to south later Tuesday into Wednesday morning. Global\nweather models already have pretty good agreement on timing of the\ntrough, with the large majority of ensemble members agreeing on\nwidespread rain showers. There are some location differences in\nthe trough placement which is leading to differences in how much\nprecipitation exactly, but at this range precipitation is looking\nlight to locally moderate. Temperatures will continue to decrease\nthrough Wed-Thu, with highs in the 60s-70s for the coastal\nbasin/high deserts, 40-55 for the mountains, and mid 70s in the\nlow deserts. Breezy onshore winds are likely for Tuesday into\nWednesday in the usual areas (coastal waters, mountains and desert\nfoothills). Keep your eye on the forecast this upcoming week. \n\nModels diverge Friday into next weekend, but general agreement is in\nsome strength of large-scale mid-level troughing continuing, so \nwhile any precip will likely taper off by late next Thursday, \ntemperatures look to remain in the cool to average zone through \nnext week.",
